My Journey of Building and Hosting an Analytics API

Objective
I built and hosted a REST API using FastAPI, tested it locally, version-controlled it with Git, and successfully published it on GitHub. Here’s a step-by-step breakdown of what I accomplished.

Step-by-Step Process
1. Setting Up My Project
	1. Created the Project Folder:
		○ I started by creating a folder named analytics_api to house all my project files.
	2. Set Up a Python Virtual Environment:
		○ I created a virtual environment using the following command:
python -m venv venv
		○ I then activated it:
			§ Windows: .\venv\Scripts\activate
			§ Mac/Linux: source venv/bin/activate
	3. Installed Dependencies:
		○ I installed the required packages:
pip install fastapi uvicorn
		
		○ To save these dependencies, I generated a requirements.txt file:

pip freeze > requirements.txt
	4. Created Project Files:
		○ I structured my project into separate files for better modularity:
			§ main.py: The entry point of the application.
			§ database.py, models.py, and schemas.py: For organizing the code base.

2. Writing the API Code
	1. Wrote the API Code in main.py:
		○ I created a simple API endpoint to fetch and display some in-memory items:

fromfastapi importFastAPI, HTTPException

app = FastAPI()

# In-memory storage for itemsitems = {
    1: {"name": "Item One", "description": "This is the first item."},
    2: {"name": "Item Two", "description": "This is the second item."},
}

@app.get("/items/")defget_items():
    returnitems
	2. Tested the Code Locally:
		○ I ran the server using the command:

uvicorn main:app --reload
		○ I accessed the API endpoints at:
			§ http://127.0.0.1:8000/items/
			§ http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs for the interactive Swagger UI.

3. Debugging and Testing
	1. Resolved Route and Port Issues:
		○ I encountered some challenges with route registration and port conflicts. To troubleshoot:
			§ I verified registered routes using:

print(app.routes)
			§ I identified and killed processes blocking the port:

netstat -ano | findstr :8000
taskkill /PID <pid_number> /F
	2. Validated the API:
		○ Once everything was running smoothly, I tested my /items/ route to ensure it returned the expected JSON response.

4. Version Controlling My Code with Git
	1. Initialized a Git Repository:
		○ I initialized the repository:

git init
		○ I staged and committed my changes:


git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit with FastAPI setup"
	2. Created a .gitignore File:
		○ I added files and folders to ignore, like:
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

5. Hosting My Project on GitHub
	1. Created a Repository on GitHub:
		○ I created a new repository named Analytics-API on GitHub.
	2. Added the Remote Repository:
		○ I linked my local repository to GitHub:
git remote add origin https://github.com/<MyUsername>/<RepositoryName>.git
	3. Pushed My Code:
		○ After some trial and error, I successfully pushed my code:
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

6. Overcoming Challenges
	1. Authentication Issues:
		○ Initially, I struggled with SSH and HTTPS authentication. I generated a Personal Access Token for GitHub and used it to authenticate successfully.
	2. Remote Repository Conflicts:
		○ I encountered a conflict because the remote repository already contained a README.md. I resolved this by pulling the remote changes first:
		○ git pull origin main --rebase
		○ After resolving any conflicts, I pushed my code again:
git push -u origin main

7. Documenting My Project
	1. Created a README.md:
		○ I wrote a detailed README to explain my project, including:
			§ A project description.
			§ Installation steps.
			§ Usage instructions.
			§ API documentation.
